1) 从告警标签取 mountpoint 与 server，执行 nfsstat:
1) Take mountpoint and server from the alert labels, run nfsstat -c to confirm the retransmission counters, and check for packet loss to the server with mtr <server>;
2) If the network is clean, run nfsstat -s and iostat -x 1 on the NFS server to see whether its disks or thread count (RPCNFSDCOUNT) are saturated;
3) UDP mounts amplify retransmissions on lossy networks, so confirm the mount options use proto=tcp;
4) Tuning the timeo/retrans mount options only relieves the symptom; the network or server bottleneck must be fixed first.

1) 从告警标签取 mountpoint、server 与 nfsstat_op:
1) Take mountpoint, server, and nfsstat_operation from the alert labels and first determine whether READ or WRITE is slow;
2) Run mountstats <mountpoint> to see the RTT distribution per operation and compare it with the baseline network latency from ping <server>;
3) If network latency is normal, run iostat -x 1 on the server to see whether the backing storage is saturated or another client is running heavy IO;
4) For slow reads consider raising rsize/wsize and enabling actimeo caching; for slow writes check whether the export uses the sync option, which is very expensive.

1) 从告警标签取 mountpoint，执行 mountstats \u003:
1) Take mountpoint from the alert labels and run mountstats <mountpoint> to compare RTT with execute; a large gap means heavy queuing;
2) Heavy queuing usually means concurrent IO exceeds the available RPC slots; raise the mount's nconnect (NFSv4.1+) or the kernel's sunrpc.tcp_slot_table_entries;
3) At the same time check whether a client process is doing large numbers of small-file operations; batching or local caching cuts IOPS dramatically;
4) If RTT is high as well, deal with the server and network via the RTT alert first.
